{
  "families": [
    {
      "family": "pipelined",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 12, "high": 18, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 10, "high": 18, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -1, "high": 3, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 3, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]}
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.35,
      "why": "One register per micro-rotation gives the shortest per-stage combinational path and therefore the highest Fmax of my two families. The 32-request burst must drain in <=0.5 us, so the batch deadline (31 + N + 2 cycles) is the binding constraint; this family is the safe corner that can still meet it at larger N. It also anchors the accuracy/area reference against which pipelined_m's register savings are judged."
    },
    {
      "family": "pipelined_m",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 12, "high": 18, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 10, "high": 18, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -1, "high": 3, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 3, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]},
        {"param": "m", "low": 2, "high": 8, "choices": null}
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.65,
      "why": "Same 1 result/cycle throughput and identical bit-accurate accuracy as pipelined, but only ceil(N/m) register banks instead of N, so it should dominate on area_um2 (the objective) whenever m is large enough to keep Fmax above the batch requirement. m is the key trade lever: larger m cuts FF area and shortens latency (ceil(N/m)+2), but lengthens the critical path (m chained adders), so the search must find the largest m that still clears the 0.5 us p99 batch bound with margin for the L2 re-simulation. This family gets the larger share because it is the most likely source of the area-minimising front."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The spec's binding constraint is the system one: a 1 MHz tick dumps 32 requests at once and all 32 must return within 0.5 us (p99). Both of my allowed families are 1-result/cycle, so a burst drains in roughly 31 + latency cycles; with latency = N+2 (pipelined) or ceil(N/m)+2 (pipelined_m) this demands Fmax of roughly 64-95 MHz depending on N and m, well above the trivial throughput_msps >= 32 floor (which only needs Fmax >= 32 MHz). Accuracy max_abs_err <= 2^-12 needs output LSB 2^-(W-2) at or below ~2^-12, i.e. W >= ~13-14, plus enough micro-rotations (N >= ~13) and/or angle_guard to kill the residual-angle error. Area is minimised by pushing W and N down to the accuracy boundary and, for pipelined_m, by pushing m up to the Fmax boundary. I therefore search W 12..18 and N 10..18 (straddling the expected feasible boundary so code can locate it exactly), angle_guard -1..3 and frac_guard 0..3 to let the search trade guard bits against W/N, both rounding modes, and m 2..8. Round 1 is broad exploration over these ranges; later rounds should refine around the feasible front and the reserved tail should map the front over the full W/N/m ranges of whichever family lands on it. I deliberately keep some Fmax margin in the front because the L1 batch bound is optimistic and the cheapest designs that only just pass it may be dropped at L2."
}
